        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>The purpose of this first back pain study was to determine whether the head-down tilt (HDT) bed rest model would elicit back pain and spinal lengthening similar to that in astronauts during space flight.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR> Eight male subjects, aged 38±6 years with an average weight of 80.8±10.4 kg and height of 182.6±5.5 cm volunteered for a 21 day study.  There was a 4-day control period, 16 days of HDT to simulate microgravity and 1 day of recovery standing upright.  Subject heights were measured on 2 control days, the first 8 and last two days of bed rest.  All subjects completed a pain questionnaire daily.  The questionnaire asked the subjects to rate the back pain on a scale of 1-5, note any activity that eased or worsened the back pain and to describe sleep patterns.
<P>
RESULTS:<br> There was an increase of 2.1±0.5 cm by the 3rd day of HDT and no further increase.  An increase in height during the night after a day of normal ambulation was less than the increase on the 3rd day of HDT. The intensity of back pain in the lower back increased to 2.5 (dull pain) and then decreased to basal levels by the 9th day.   
<P>
Although spinal lengthening in space is twice that experienced during HDT, the type, level, distribution and time course of back pain during HDT was similar to that experienced during space flight. The pain subsided when the spinal lengthening stopped. Astronauts report dull back pain between days 1 and 6 of space flight and it is noted that a knees to chest position reduces the pain in astronauts as well as in volunteers in this study.
        </div>

